
Yes. A nice way is to use two absolutely/fixed-positioned SVG <img> elements—one on the left and one on the right—and control their transparency with CSS.

Simple approach


<body>
  <img class="page-decoration left" src="left-decoration.svg" alt="">
  <img class="page-decoration right" src="right-decoration.svg" alt="">

  <main class="content">
    <h1>My Page</h1>
    <p>Your page content goes here...</p>
  </main>
</body>

body {
  margin: 0;
  min-height: 100vh;
  position: relative;
  background: #fff;
}

.page-decoration {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  width: 180px;
  height: 100vh;

  opacity: 0.25;
  pointer-events: none;

  z-index: -1;
}

.page-decoration.left {
  left: 0;
}

.page-decoration.right {
  right: 0;
}

.content {
  position: relative;
  max-width: 900px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 60px 40px;
}
≈=======================
If you want the SVG to fade toward the content
You can use a CSS mask/gradient effect. For example, the left decoration can gradually disappear toward the center:


.page-decoration.left {
  left: 0;

  -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(
    to right,
    black 0%,
    black 50%,
    transparent 100%
  );

  mask-image: linear-gradient(
    to right,
    black 0%,
    black 50%,
    transparent 100%
  );
}

.page-decoration.right {
  right: 0;

  -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(
    to left,
    black 0%,
    black 50%,
    transparent 100%
  );

  mask-image: linear-gradient(
    to left,
    black 0%,
    black 50%,
    transparent 100%
  );
}




===============
Important: mobile screens
You probably don't want the decorations covering your content on phones:



@media (max-width: 700px) {
  .page-decoration {
    width: 80px;
    opacity: 0.15;
  }
}


@media (max-width: 500px) {
  .page-decoration {
    display: none;
  }
}




